ries study of 1958  which was an epidemiological  longitudinal study  meaning it measured the risk of heart  disease in a large population  over a long period of time while the  seven countries studies  did find an association between  saturated fat and heart disease  it has been heavily criticized even to  this day  critics have pointed out that keys  cherry-picked data from countries based  on desired outcomes  and excluded countries like france for  example which have higher intakes of  saturated fat  the study also didn't distinguish  between saturated fat and trans fats  and it also didn't consider sugar as a  potential contributor  there have since been a lot of other  better studies that have found an  association between saturated fat intake  and heart disease including but not  limited to the ireland boston heart  study  the honolulu heart program and the  atherosclerosis  risk of community study while these  studies all conclude that saturated fat  intake  is associated with increased risk of  coronary heart disease  they also found that low intakes of  polyunsaturated fats  and high fiber complex carbohydrates  were also associated with increased  risk similarly a recent 2020  meta-analysis  concluded that reducing saturated fat  intakes lowered the risk of  cardiovascular disease by 21  but this reduction had little effect on  the risk of dying  the review likewise suggests that  replacing saturated fats with  polyunsaturated fats and healthy carbs  may also be beneficial  there have also been other recent  meta-analysis that add  tons of confusion the 2014 publication  in the annals of internal medicine found  that there was no association between  saturated fat and heart disease but this  study was  heavily critiqued for its poor research  methods there was also more recent 2015  canadian meta-analysis and systematic  review of 12 prospective cohort studies  where the authors similarly concluded  that saturated fat  does not appear to be significantly 