o  effect from eating better  it also mentioned that including more  unsaturated fats  have positive health benefits so does  that mean you should go to town and eat  more butter probably not i mean  too much of anything isn't necessarily  good for you and eating  more saturated fat which has either a  negative  or potentially a neutral effect  depending on which study you read  should not really be the goal rather  what it says to me is according to the  research  it may mean that enjoying a little  butter on your mashed potatoes  may not have the overwhelming negative  effect on heart health  that we once believed so i know i know  who to believe on all this right  nutrition research is notoriously  rife with confounders and  inconsistencies and of course  the evidence on saturated fat is no  different  but to me it comes down to a really  important  question what has a greater impact on  heart disease  high intakes of saturated fats or low  intakes of polyunsaturated fats and  complex carbohydrates  by looking at the body of evidence  considerations around saturated fat and  the risk of heart disease  are really two-fold one saturated fat  in isolation may appear to play a role  in heart disease by increasing heart  disease risk factors  but depending on which research you read  it seems like it may  or may not increase the risk of death  from heart disease itself  shifting the focus to consuming more  unsaturated fats  seems to have a more clear beneficial  effect in the literature  with that said we should be less  concerned with the total saturated fat  intake  and more concerned with emphasizing  greater intakes  of unsaturated fats specifically  polyunsaturated fats  like omega-3s and two replacing  saturated fats with high fiber  carbohydrates  but not refined carbohydrates should be  the other goal  as fiber specifically soluble fiber  has demonstrated a clear benefit towards  reducing cholesterol levels  and improving overall heart health now  while the evidence is  mixed as to whether or not saturated fat  actually causes harm  and to what extent what we do know is  that saturated fat also  doesn't seem to provide much benefit to  our heart either  so if we focus instead on adding more of  the clearly beneficial heart-healthy  foods like unsaturated fats  and higher fiber carbs which have shown  a clear benefit  it will naturally kind of edge out any  potential negative health effects of  saturated fat  now with that said what's the deal with  coconut oil  well marketing of coconut oil has  exploded in recent  years which can of course give people  the impression  that this is really really good for you  but we also know that coconut oil is  high in saturated fat  in fact to compare one tablespoon of  other sources of saturated fat like  butter or ghee or cream  all contain less than 10 grams of  saturated fat per tablespoon  whereas one tablespoon of coconut oil  contains around 12 to 13 grams of  saturated fat  so will coconut oil put you at risk for  heart disease  or is it actually beneficial well  we don't know for sure research on  coconut oil  is admittedly mixed however most quality  research suggests that coconut oil can  in fact raise bad ldl cholesterol  while also raising good hdl cholesterol  interestingly despite being really high  in saturated fats  some research suggests that it may not  raise ldl cholesterol  as much as other saturated fats so  it is possible that the net effect isn't  bad for instance one 2017 randomized  control trial  compared the effects of butter coconut  oil and olive oil  on the cholesterol levels of 96 men and  women  with no previous history of chronic  disease  despite being wildly different in  saturated fat content  there are no differences in the ldl  levels of those fed coconut oil  versus olive oil in contrast butter did  significantly increase ldl cholesterol  compared to coconut oil which was  surprising considering that coconut oil  is actually higher in saturated