ng  delicious plant-based sources can  include things like chia seeds  walnuts flax seeds and hemp hearts  number five eat more  high fiber beans and legumes beans and  other pulses are not only  great plant-based proteins but they're  rich in resistant starch and fiber  which research suggests may help to  reduce bad ldl cholesterol  so throw a handful of chickpeas into  your salad your white beans in your soup  black beans and your tacos and lentils  in your curry for some heart healthy  and of course money conscious meals  number six eat more plant-based fats  like nuts  seeds and avocado as a fantastic source  of monounsaturated fats and fiber  nut seeds and avocado can be your heart  healthy bffs  some nuts and seeds like walnuts flax or  hemp hearts  even have those plant-based omega-3s we  were just talking about  so research has found that eating nuts  reduced the risk of heart disease  while another study found that those who  ate avocados regularly  were half as likely to develop metabolic  syndrome  number seven eat the rainbow when it  comes to eating vegetables and fruit  color is king there are a number of  important antioxidants that can be  derived from  brightly colored veggies so lycopene and  tomatoes for example which i know is a  fruit  has been linked to a reduced risk of  heart disease and stroke  while alpha and beta-carotene in orange  carrots or yellow beets  have been shown to lower the risk of  death from heart disease  there are so many delicious ways to get  your colorful veggie fix  from peppers to squash to pumpkins and  beets  so choose a favorite and get cooking  number eight is to swap some of your  meat for soy protein to make some more  meatless  meals soy protein found in tofu tempeh  edamame and soy milk  is packed with soy isoflavones which  research suggests  may help to reduce total cholesterol and  bad ldl cholesterol  try switching your cream for soy in your  coffee throwing some tofu into your stir  fry  or adding edamame